 "intro": "The pass of Thermopylae, Greece, 191 BC. King Antiochus III of the Seleucid empire holds the pass with a wall, and his Aetolian allies hold the heights. You are the consul Manius Acilius Glabrio; your legate is Marcus Porcius Cato.",
 "debrief": "In Livy’s and Plutarch’s accounts, Acilius attacked the wall from the front while Cato led a force by night over the mountain, as the Persians had done in 480 BC. Cato surprised the Aetolians on the heights and came down behind the Seleucid army, which broke; Antiochus escaped with about 500 horsemen and soon left Greece. The Romans followed him to Asia and beat him at Magnesia. The other choices are what-ifs.",
